1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,500
Consumer vs Archival Microfilm Scanners. What is the difference, and which do you need?

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:11,833
A consumer film scanner digitises a few frames at a time.

3
00:00:11,833 --> 00:00:21,073
An archival (production) microfilm scanner transports whole 16 mm and 35 mm rolls and microfiche, with consistent capture and indexed, quality-checked output.

4
00:00:21,073 --> 00:00:28,500
For a records or heritage collection, a consumer scanner cannot keep quality or metadata consistent across thousands of frames.

5
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:37,384
: Film handled. Consumer scanner: Slides, short strips. Archival scanner: 16 mm and 35 mm rolls, fiche. : Transport. Consumer scanner: Manual, frame by frame.

6
00:00:37,384 --> 00:00:45,568
Archival scanner: Motorised, tension control. : Frame detection. Consumer scanner: Manual cropping. Archival scanner: Automatic detection and deskew. : Consistency.

7
00:00:45,568 --> 00:00:52,005
Consumer scanner: Settings vary. Archival scanner: Fixed resolution and exposure. : Output. Consumer scanner: Single image files.

8
00:00:52,005 --> 00:00:54,500
Archival scanner: Batch TIFF, PDF, PDF/A with OCR.

9
00:00:55,500 --> 00:01:05,236
FADGI notes microfilm is digitised at a fixed ppi, whatever the original reduction ratio. The original document's scale is lost unless it is recorded in metadata.

10
00:01:05,236 --> 00:01:14,671
FADGI grades image quality on a 1 to 4 star scale aligned with ISO 19264. Production scanners are calibrated against test targets, so output can be measured.

11
00:01:14,671 --> 00:01:20,500
Source: FADGI Technical Guidelines for Digitizing Cultural Heritage Materials, 3rd edition (2023)

12
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:30,140
Converting whole rolls or fiche sets for a records, library or heritage collection. Output that must be searchable, indexed and quality-checked.

13
00:01:30,140 --> 00:01:39,500
Records that may be produced as evidence, where the capture process must be documented. Old or fragile film, including acetate affected by vinegar syndrome.

14
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:49,352
1. Reader-scanner: on-demand lookup and printing in a reading room. 2. Production scanner: in-house conversion of whole collections. 3.

15
00:01:49,352 --> 00:01:52,500
Scanning bureau: when there is no in-house team.

16
00:01:53,500 --> 00:02:02,110
Can I use a flatbed scanner? It can capture single frames, but has no roll transport or frame detection. Not practical for whole rolls.

17
00:02:02,110 --> 00:02:11,995
Should I keep the film after scanning? Yes, if it is in good condition. Many archives keep it as the preservation master. Can a bureau handle acetate film?

18
00:02:11,995 --> 00:02:18,500
A specialist can assess condition first. Badly deteriorated film may need duplication before scanning.

19
